/* ============================================================================================
   chapter: shell. The optional bottom plate (Brock, F12.10): solid outside, triangles inside.
     pltPlate  the plate, outside and in: in 3D the shell's own plate turns over; in 2D the same layout, drawn
     pltSim    the same weight three ways, pressed at the lens: a solid plate, a plain grid, Theseus (better at a glance)
   The layout comes from the shell's base (3d/shell/tools/plate_layout.py): an X from the lens ring to the four screw bosses,
   the keel, two ties along the screw lines, two side spokes and a wall round the edge close twelve triangular bays, and a
   frequency-3 subdivision (as a geodesic dome is cut) fills each bay with copies of itself. Plate v2 (F20.3): every rib is
   2.3 mm tall on a 0.60 mm skin and no rib is wider than the skin (0.35 to 0.6 mm); the lens hub ring and the ties, which
   the FE wants wider, are twin walls. Its skates (F20.1, F20.4): a half-moon at each end after the Viper V4 Pro's rear skate,
   on the plate alone, with a cutout round each corner dot (the dots stay on the rim's ledges). The 3D part is meshed from
   the same layout (plate_mesh.mjs) into shell-theseus-1seam.glb.
   The FE (a grillage of the ribs as T-beams on DKT plate triangles for the skin, written for this plate because the deck's
   frame solver carries loads in its own plane, and a plate pressed at the lens bends out of it) was run on the three plates
   at the same weight (PLT_FE.mass); PLT_FE holds its results, and the slide draws them.
   ============================================================================================ */
Object.assign(SRC, {
  plt_fe: { t: 'This deck: plate FE of the optional bottom plate, three designs at the same 4.20 g (PP-C-20A, 13.5 GPa, nu 0.35), no rib wider than the 0.60 mm skin: a solid 1.06 mm plate; a plain grid (0.60 mm skin, 8 mm square grid of 0.57 x 2.3 mm ribs, the same edge wall, the same twin-walled lens ring and bosses); Theseus (0.60 mm skin, X, keel, ties and edge walls sized by stress, the lens ring and ties as twin walls, frequency-3 triangle lattice, all 2.3 mm tall). Skin as DKT triangles (checked against Timoshenko, within 1 %), ribs as T-beams (flange w + 12 t). Held at the four screw bosses; 10 N on the lens ring. Lens flex 400 / 80 / 88 um, peak stress 26.6 / 14.5 / 13.0 MPa; 10 N on the front skate: 2165 / 306 / 338 um', u: '', n: 'Masses count the skin and the ribs without their fillets, the same way for all three; the drawn Theseus part, fillets and all, is 4.30 g. Peak stress is the highest member or skin-triangle value outside the screw bosses. v1 (0.40 mm skin, struts to 1.1 mm, 3.40 g): 751 / 114 / 113 um, 40.4 / 18.8 / 14.0 MPa.' },
  plt_brock: { t: 'Brock Liddicoat, 2026-11-04 (F12.10): "i like the X style designs, but they have to form triangles"; "a fine triangle lattice, but you can only see it looking from inside the shell, on the outside it should look solid. also the bottom should be solid with no holes (even before mylar)"', u: '' },
  plt_strength: { t: 'Mitsubishi Chemical, Pyrofil PP-C-20A: tensile strength 120 MPa (the break the deck uses, F12.12)', u: '' }
});
